INT. ORBITAL ELEVATOR CAR - ASCENDING - NIGHT

A glass cylinder rising through the dark. The Earth is a shrinking blue plate below. Warning lights pulse amber. The car shudders.

Six passengers: CAPTAIN LEILA NOURI, pilot suit, grips the rail. ENGINEER BODHI RAMASWAMY, welding goggles on his forehead, kneels at an open panel. DR. WANJIRU KARIUKI, in a lab coat, hugs a sealed sample case. SECURITY OFFICER DMITRI VOLKOV-STERN, riot vest, gun holstered, scans the others. A CHILD, 8, in a pink vest. And a SILENT MONK, bald, saffron robes, eyes closed.

LEILA
Bodhi, talk to me.

BODHI
Cable's fraying. We have twenty minutes.

DR. KARIUKI
Twenty minutes to what?

BODHI
To snap.

DMITRI
Then we go down?

LEILA
No. Too fast.

BODHI
We can't go up either.

DR. KARIUKI
Why not?

BODHI
Because up is thirty-five thousand kilometers, Doctor.

The car jolts. The child cries out. The monk's eyes open. He rises, steps to the child, and puts a hand on her shoulder. Dmitri draws his weapon, thinks better of it, holsters it. Dr. Kariuki sets down the sample case and unzips her lab coat. Leila slams a fist on the panel; the lights go out; the car drops half a meter; Bodhi swears and jams his wrench into the gap.

LEILA
Bodhi!

BODHI
I know!

DMITRI
Captain, I can climb the cable.

LEILA
Dmitri, nobody climbs a cable.

DMITRI
Then what?

The monk raises one finger. Everyone stops. He points up. Above them, in the black, a second car is descending, lights blazing.
